outputs.exact_statement:
There are positive weights $W_\lambda$, an absolute finite $p_*$, and real exponents $2\le p_d\le p_*$ such that, for every $d\ge1$ and every $\lambda\vdash2^d$, $$\label{eq:weighted-main}
 D_\lambda^{3/4}\le W_\lambda\le D_\lambda,
 \qquad W_\lambda\|K_d(\lambda)\|_{p_d}^{p_d}\le1.$$ Consequently $\|K_d(\lambda)\|_{\rm op}\le D_\lambda^{-3/(4p_*)}$.

hypotheses.statement:
There are absolute constants $M,P_s$ such that, for all sufficiently large dyadic $n$, $$\label{eq:sparse-contribution}
 \sum_{\substack{\lambda\vdash n\\1\le n-\lambda_1\le n e^{-M\sqrt{\log n}}}}
 D_\lambda\operatorname{Tr}_{V_\lambda}|Q_n(\lambda)|^{P_s}
 \le n^{-12}.$$

With the notation above, put $$M_*=\sum_{t\le T}\binom nt.$$ Then $$n!\|P_*\Delta_*e\|^2
 \le n!M_*^2\max_{0\le t\le T}
 \frac{\dim(\operatorname{Sym}^{n-t}W_0)}
      {t!\,(n-t)^{n-t}}.$$

